  i This if statement compares the same value as the previous one, but against a different value.
  
    3 │ {
    4 │ 	if (x === 1) if (y) 0;
  > 5 │ 	if (x === 2) console.log("two");
      │ 	^^^^^^^^^^^^
    6 │ }
    7 │ 
  
  i The previous condition is here.
  
    2 │ // An `else` after an `if` without its own `else` would belong to that inner `if`.
    3 │ {
  > 4 │ 	if (x === 1) if (y) 0;
      │ 	    ^^^^^^^
    5 │ 	if (x === 2) console.log("two");
    6 │ }
  
  i Only one of these conditions can be true, but separate if statements hide this, and the second condition is still checked when the first one matched.
  
  i Add else before this if statement to join it to the previous one.
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

